keep the shell out of the frame, and the blue-light filter in the mask

Two things this backend was paying for every frame in a GBA game, found by
arming the latency trace on a Mini Plus and reading what it said rather than
guessing which stage was slow.

**The Advance SP shell was being blitted over the game, every frame.**
Upstream changed how it reaches the screen in 9d2bc24: it used to be handed
to the compositor as an image, which `0013` paints once into the surround,
and now it is uploaded as a texture and drawn in the display list. Nothing
calls `set_bezel` any more, so `0013` was quietly bypassed and this port has
been paying a 640x480 blit since the previous bump -- 5.0-5.5 ms a frame,
measured -- and losing the direct path with it, because the list was no
longer just the game.

`draw_list` now recognises `[Game, Tex]` where the texture covers the canvas
and is seated at the top, takes it into the surround, and draws the game
alone. While the strip is *moving* -- the eject animation slides it -- it
stays in the frame, because a moving thing belongs there. The list reads
[Game, Tex] and not the other way round: the shell is drawn over the game,
and matching it backwards silently does nothing.

**Blue light cost a whole extra pass over the panel.** The gain was applied
when the canvas was copied out, and the direct path was skipped whenever it
was on, so the filter turned a 5 ms frame into 18 ms and dropped three
quarters of them.

It now rides in the column mask. The game pass already multiplies every
column by an 8.8 factor -- the LCD3x grid and the brightness -- and the gain
is folded into that when the table is built, which happens when the rect
moves rather than per frame. The surround's shell is graded once as it is
taken. Everything reaching the panel is then already warm, so the direct path
runs with the filter on and blue light costs nothing per frame:

| | before | after |
|---|---|---|
| filter on | work 18.2 ms, 451/600 missed, out 7.9 | work 6.1 ms, 101/600, out 5.0 |
| filter off | work 5.9 ms, 26/600 missed | unchanged |

Three traps, each with a guard, because this is a flag that has to agree with
itself in two places and today produced two bugs of exactly that shape:

  - **Double-grading.** The mask carries the gain only when the pass is bound
    for the panel; the canvas path leaves it at 256 and `output_frame` grades
    that copy as before. The destination is part of the column table's key,
    so changing path rebuilds it.
  - **Stale warmth.** The gain is in the key too, and the shell remembers
    which gain it was graded with, so moving the filter rebuilds both.
  - **Wrong channel.** The mask is in panel byte order, byte 0 blue, and the
    test asserts each channel equals the table applied exactly once.

One route was tried and rejected: applying a lookup to the rows as they were
written to the panel. The destination is mmapped /dev/fb0, so the table has
to read each byte back, and a read from framebuffer memory is not a read from
RAM -- 160-200 ms a frame against 4-5 for the same loop over the canvas. That
number is in the code as a comment, because it looks like a free optimisation
every time you meet it.

The graded copy on the canvas path is a straight row walk with the lookups
folded in, rather than falling to the general per-pixel path: 24 ms a frame
to 8 for the frames that still take it, which is menus and the shelf.

Applies to upstream 91499a9.

